1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,190
Almost four hundred years ago,

2
00:00:02,190 --> 00:00:07,290
a scientist called Jan van Helmont
planted a small willow tree in a pot of

3
00:00:07,290 --> 00:00:08,520
dried soil.

4
00:00:08,520 --> 00:00:10,380
He weighed the tree.

5
00:00:10,380 --> 00:00:12,240
He weighed the soil.

6
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:14,080
Then, for five years,

7
00:00:14,080 --> 00:00:18,640
he gave it nothing but
water. The tree grew.

8
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:22,910
It gained about seventy-four
kilograms. And the soil?

9
00:00:22,910 --> 00:00:26,140
It had lost only about
fifty-seven grams.

10
00:00:26,840 --> 00:00:32,800
So where did a whole tree's worth
of mass come from? Not the soil.

11
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:35,720
Most of it came out of thin air.

12
00:00:36,120 --> 00:00:38,150
By the end of this video,

13
00:00:38,150 --> 00:00:39,900
you'll know exactly how,

14
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:42,870
and how to explain it in an exam.

15
00:00:46,220 --> 00:00:49,790
You eat to get your
food. Plants don't.

16
00:00:49,790 --> 00:00:51,830
Plants make their own food,

17
00:00:51,830 --> 00:00:53,510
by photosynthesis.

18
00:00:53,910 --> 00:00:57,190
Here's the definition
the mark scheme wants.

19
00:00:57,190 --> 00:00:59,590
Learn it word for word.

20
00:00:59,590 --> 00:01:03,950
Photosynthesis is the process by
which plants synthesise carbohydrates

21
00:01:03,950 --> 00:01:07,330
from raw materials
using energy from light.

22
00:01:08,230 --> 00:01:10,140
Let's break that down.

23
00:01:10,140 --> 00:01:12,010
Synthesise means make.

24
00:01:12,010 --> 00:01:15,370
The carbohydrate a plant
makes first is glucose,

25
00:01:15,370 --> 00:01:16,890
a simple sugar.

26
00:01:16,890 --> 00:01:20,460
The raw materials are
carbon dioxide and water.

27
00:01:20,460 --> 00:01:23,920
And the energy to do
it comes from light.

28
00:01:24,870 --> 00:01:26,830
Now the word equation.

29
00:01:26,830 --> 00:01:28,970
Carbon dioxide, plus water,

30
00:01:28,970 --> 00:01:31,470
gives glucose, plus oxygen.

31
00:01:31,770 --> 00:01:35,710
And it happens in the presence
of light and chlorophyll,

32
00:01:35,710 --> 00:01:38,390
so we write those on the arrow.

33
00:01:38,390 --> 00:01:41,090
They're conditions, not reactants.

34
00:01:41,090 --> 00:01:42,840
Light isn't a substance,

35
00:01:42,840 --> 00:01:45,340
and chlorophyll isn't used up.

36
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:49,060
So where do the raw
materials come from?

37
00:01:49,060 --> 00:01:52,920
Carbon dioxide diffuses
into the leaf from the air.

38
00:01:52,920 --> 00:01:56,220
Water is absorbed from
the soil by the roots,

39
00:01:56,220 --> 00:01:58,110
and travels up the stem,

40
00:01:58,110 --> 00:02:00,740
in the xylem, to the leaf.

41
00:02:01,040 --> 00:02:03,290
Oxygen is a waste product.

42
00:02:03,290 --> 00:02:06,400
Some is used in the
plant's own respiration,

43
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:10,780
and the rest diffuses out
of the leaf, into the air.

44
00:02:11,730 --> 00:02:14,260
If you're taking the Extended paper,

45
00:02:14,260 --> 00:02:17,840
you also need the
balanced symbol equation.

46
00:02:17,840 --> 00:02:21,180
Six C O two, plus six H two O,

47
00:02:21,180 --> 00:02:23,850
gives C six H twelve O six,

48
00:02:23,850 --> 00:02:25,710
plus six O two.

49
00:02:26,210 --> 00:02:28,220
Let's check it balances.

50
00:02:28,220 --> 00:02:30,380
Carbon: six on the left,

51
00:02:30,380 --> 00:02:32,090
six on the right.

52
00:02:32,090 --> 00:02:34,810
Hydrogen: twelve on each side.

53
00:02:34,810 --> 00:02:38,680
Oxygen: twelve plus six
makes eighteen on the left,

54
00:02:38,680 --> 00:02:43,770
and six plus twelve makes eighteen
on the right. It balances.

55
00:02:44,720 --> 00:02:47,940
So, what does the light actually do?

56
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:51,660
Inside the cells of a
leaf are chloroplasts.

57
00:02:51,660 --> 00:02:53,820
Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll,

58
00:02:53,820 --> 00:02:55,690
a green pigment.

59
00:02:55,690 --> 00:02:57,760
Chlorophyll absorbs light.

60
00:02:57,760 --> 00:03:01,950
Then it transfers energy from
light into energy in chemicals,

61
00:03:01,950 --> 00:03:04,650
for the synthesis of carbohydrates.

62
00:03:04,650 --> 00:03:09,040
In other words, that energy
ends up stored in glucose.

63
00:03:09,540 --> 00:03:12,280
Be careful with your wording here.

64
00:03:12,280 --> 00:03:15,420
Never write that
chlorophyll makes energy.

65
00:03:15,420 --> 00:03:17,280
Energy can't be made.

66
00:03:17,280 --> 00:03:19,300
Chlorophyll transfers it.

67
00:03:19,300 --> 00:03:22,510
So use the syllabus word: transfers.

68
00:03:23,460 --> 00:03:26,000
So the plant has made glucose.

69
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:27,810
What happens to it?

70
00:03:27,810 --> 00:03:30,700
There are five uses to learn.

71
00:03:30,700 --> 00:03:33,730
One. Glucose is used in respiration,

72
00:03:33,730 --> 00:03:36,810
to release energy for
the plant's cells.

73
00:03:36,810 --> 00:03:39,790
Two. Glucose is turned into starch,

74
00:03:39,790 --> 00:03:41,600
as an energy store.

75
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:43,110
Starch is insoluble,

76
00:03:43,110 --> 00:03:47,650
so storing it doesn't affect the
movement of water by osmosis.

77
00:03:47,650 --> 00:03:51,020
Three. Glucose is used
to make cellulose,

78
00:03:51,020 --> 00:03:53,370
which builds the cell walls.

79
00:03:53,370 --> 00:03:56,500
Four. Glucose is
changed into sucrose,

80
00:03:56,500 --> 00:03:58,590
for transport in the phloem,

81
00:03:58,590 --> 00:04:02,160
to other parts of
the plant. And five.

82
00:04:02,160 --> 00:04:04,490
Sugars are used to make nectar,

83
00:04:04,490 --> 00:04:08,260
in flowers, to attract
insects for pollination.

84
00:04:08,760 --> 00:04:10,890
Respiration. Starch.

85
00:04:10,890 --> 00:04:14,010
Cellulose. Sucrose. Nectar.

86
00:04:14,960 --> 00:04:17,300
But glucose only contains carbon,

87
00:04:17,300 --> 00:04:19,070
hydrogen and oxygen.

88
00:04:19,070 --> 00:04:21,980
To grow, a plant also needs proteins,

89
00:04:21,980 --> 00:04:25,060
and proteins are made
from amino acids.

90
00:04:25,060 --> 00:04:27,670
Amino acids contain nitrogen.

91
00:04:27,670 --> 00:04:30,830
So plants absorb nitrate
ions from the soil,

92
00:04:30,830 --> 00:04:32,600
through their roots.

93
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:36,020
Nitrate ions are needed
to make amino acids.

94
00:04:36,020 --> 00:04:37,680
Without enough nitrate,

95
00:04:37,680 --> 00:04:39,820
the plant has stunted growth,

96
00:04:39,820 --> 00:04:42,610
and its older leaves turn yellow.

97
00:04:43,110 --> 00:04:45,760
Plants also absorb magnesium ions.

98
00:04:45,760 --> 00:04:49,190
Magnesium ions are needed
to make chlorophyll.

99
00:04:49,190 --> 00:04:50,950
Without enough magnesium,

100
00:04:50,950 --> 00:04:54,130
the leaves turn yellow
between the veins.

101
00:04:54,130 --> 00:04:57,300
Less chlorophyll means
less light is absorbed,

102
00:04:57,300 --> 00:04:59,760
so there's less photosynthesis.

103
00:05:00,260 --> 00:05:02,810
In an exam, link three things:

104
00:05:02,810 --> 00:05:05,420
the ion, what it's used to make,

105
00:05:05,420 --> 00:05:07,870
and what happens without it.

106
00:05:08,820 --> 00:05:11,700
Most photosynthesis
happens in the leaves,

107
00:05:11,700 --> 00:05:14,540
and leaves are adapted for it.

108
00:05:14,540 --> 00:05:17,130
Most leaves are broad and thin.

109
00:05:17,130 --> 00:05:19,560
Broad means a large surface area,

110
00:05:19,560 --> 00:05:21,470
to absorb more light.

111
00:05:21,470 --> 00:05:26,190
Thin means a short distance for carbon
dioxide to diffuse to the cells

112
00:05:26,190 --> 00:05:27,230
inside.

113
00:05:27,730 --> 00:05:30,020
Now let's cut a leaf open.

114
00:05:30,020 --> 00:05:34,810
This is a cross-section through
the leaf of a dicotyledonous plant.

115
00:05:34,810 --> 00:05:36,660
It's a simplified diagram,

116
00:05:36,660 --> 00:05:40,990
so let's go through it layer
by layer, from the top.

117
00:05:41,490 --> 00:05:43,740
First, the waxy cuticle.

118
00:05:43,740 --> 00:05:45,440
It reduces water loss,

119
00:05:45,440 --> 00:05:47,440
and because it's transparent,

120
00:05:47,440 --> 00:05:50,340
light passes straight through it.

121
00:05:50,340 --> 00:05:52,690
Next, the upper epidermis.

122
00:05:52,690 --> 00:05:54,980
These cells are transparent too,

123
00:05:54,980 --> 00:05:57,170
and they have no chloroplasts,

124
00:05:57,170 --> 00:06:00,840
so light passes through
to the cells below.

125
00:06:00,840 --> 00:06:03,100
Then the palisade mesophyll.

126
00:06:03,100 --> 00:06:04,750
These cells are tall,

127
00:06:04,750 --> 00:06:07,860
tightly packed, close
to the top surface,

128
00:06:07,860 --> 00:06:09,970
and full of chloroplasts.

129
00:06:09,970 --> 00:06:12,510
So they absorb the most light.

130
00:06:12,510 --> 00:06:15,750
This is where most
photosynthesis happens.

131
00:06:16,700 --> 00:06:19,980
Below the palisade is
the spongy mesophyll.

132
00:06:19,980 --> 00:06:21,680
Its cells are rounder,

133
00:06:21,680 --> 00:06:23,140
and loosely packed,

134
00:06:23,140 --> 00:06:25,540
with air spaces between them.

135
00:06:25,540 --> 00:06:29,430
The air spaces let carbon
dioxide diffuse to the cells,

136
00:06:29,430 --> 00:06:33,530
and they give a large surface
area for gas exchange.

137
00:06:34,030 --> 00:06:37,460
Running through the leaf
are vascular bundles.

138
00:06:37,460 --> 00:06:41,560
The xylem brings water and
mineral ions into the leaf.

139
00:06:41,560 --> 00:06:44,920
The phloem carries sucrose
and amino acids away,

140
00:06:44,920 --> 00:06:47,310
to the rest of the plant.

141
00:06:47,810 --> 00:06:50,680
On the underside is
the lower epidermis,

142
00:06:50,680 --> 00:06:53,180
with tiny pores called stomata.

143
00:06:53,180 --> 00:06:55,570
One pore is called a stoma.

144
00:06:55,570 --> 00:06:58,790
Carbon dioxide diffuses
in through the stomata,

145
00:06:58,790 --> 00:07:00,850
and oxygen diffuses out.

146
00:07:00,850 --> 00:07:05,380
Each stoma is opened and closed
by a pair of guard cells.

147
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:07,990
So, from top to bottom:

148
00:07:07,990 --> 00:07:09,940
cuticle, upper epidermis,

149
00:07:09,940 --> 00:07:11,310
palisade mesophyll,

150
00:07:11,310 --> 00:07:13,800
spongy mesophyll, vascular bundle,

151
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:17,280
lower epidermis with
stomata and guard cells.

152
00:07:18,430 --> 00:07:22,870
So how can you show that a leaf
has been photosynthesising?

153
00:07:22,870 --> 00:07:24,420
Test it for starch,

154
00:07:24,420 --> 00:07:28,520
because a leaf stores some
of its glucose as starch.

155
00:07:29,020 --> 00:07:31,370
First, destarch the plant.

156
00:07:31,370 --> 00:07:34,570
Put it in the dark for a day or two.

157
00:07:34,570 --> 00:07:37,490
With no light, it
can't photosynthesise,

158
00:07:37,490 --> 00:07:40,220
so it uses up its stored starch.

159
00:07:40,220 --> 00:07:44,840
That means any starch you find later
must have been made during your

160
00:07:44,840 --> 00:07:47,530
experiment. Step one:

161
00:07:47,530 --> 00:07:51,380
put the leaf in boiling
water for about a minute.

162
00:07:51,380 --> 00:07:53,030
This kills the cells,

163
00:07:53,030 --> 00:07:55,560
and breaks down the cell membranes,

164
00:07:55,560 --> 00:07:59,590
so the iodine can get
in later. Step two:

165
00:07:59,590 --> 00:08:01,890
turn off the Bunsen burner.

166
00:08:01,890 --> 00:08:05,380
Then put the leaf in a
boiling tube of ethanol,

167
00:08:05,380 --> 00:08:08,010
and stand it in the hot water.

168
00:08:08,010 --> 00:08:09,570
Ethanol is flammable,

169
00:08:09,570 --> 00:08:12,590
so never heat it with a naked flame.

170
00:08:12,590 --> 00:08:15,370
The hot ethanol removes
the chlorophyll,

171
00:08:15,370 --> 00:08:20,180
so the colour change will
be easy to see. Step three:

172
00:08:20,180 --> 00:08:22,570
dip the leaf in warm water.

173
00:08:22,570 --> 00:08:24,710
The ethanol makes it brittle,

174
00:08:24,710 --> 00:08:28,310
and the water softens it. Step four:

175
00:08:28,310 --> 00:08:30,780
spread the leaf on a white tile,

176
00:08:30,780 --> 00:08:33,100
so the colour is easy to see,

177
00:08:33,100 --> 00:08:35,260
and add iodine solution.

178
00:08:35,560 --> 00:08:37,260
If it turns blue-black,

179
00:08:37,260 --> 00:08:38,930
starch is present.

180
00:08:38,930 --> 00:08:40,730
If it stays orange-brown,

181
00:08:40,730 --> 00:08:42,450
there's no starch.

182
00:08:43,600 --> 00:08:48,090
Now you can use that test to
show what photosynthesis needs.

183
00:08:48,090 --> 00:08:50,640
Each experiment needs a control.

184
00:08:50,640 --> 00:08:53,260
A control is identical to the test,

185
00:08:53,260 --> 00:08:56,690
except for the one factor
you're investigating.

186
00:08:56,690 --> 00:09:00,740
It shows that the result
was caused by that factor.

187
00:09:01,240 --> 00:09:03,060
First, chlorophyll.

188
00:09:03,060 --> 00:09:06,360
Take a destarched plant
with variegated leaves:

189
00:09:06,360 --> 00:09:09,880
leaves that are partly
green and partly white.

190
00:09:09,880 --> 00:09:12,720
The white parts have no chlorophyll.

191
00:09:12,720 --> 00:09:15,670
Leave it in the light
for a few hours,

192
00:09:15,670 --> 00:09:17,380
then test a leaf.

193
00:09:17,380 --> 00:09:20,220
Only the green parts turn blue-black.

194
00:09:20,220 --> 00:09:22,820
The white parts stay orange-brown.

195
00:09:22,820 --> 00:09:25,030
So chlorophyll is needed.

196
00:09:25,530 --> 00:09:27,100
Second, light.

197
00:09:27,100 --> 00:09:31,420
Cover part of a leaf on a
destarched plant with black paper,

198
00:09:31,420 --> 00:09:34,920
and leave it in the
light for a few hours.

199
00:09:34,920 --> 00:09:39,220
After the test, only the
uncovered part turns blue-black.

200
00:09:39,220 --> 00:09:41,960
The uncovered part is the control.

201
00:09:41,960 --> 00:09:43,870
So light is needed.

202
00:09:44,370 --> 00:09:46,430
Third, carbon dioxide.

203
00:09:46,430 --> 00:09:49,380
Seal a leaf in a
flask with soda lime,

204
00:09:49,380 --> 00:09:51,690
which absorbs carbon dioxide.

205
00:09:51,690 --> 00:09:54,170
The control is an identical flask,

206
00:09:54,170 --> 00:09:55,930
with no soda lime.

207
00:09:55,930 --> 00:09:58,350
After a few hours in the light,

208
00:09:58,350 --> 00:10:01,290
only the control leaf
turns blue-black.

209
00:10:01,290 --> 00:10:03,740
So carbon dioxide is needed.

210
00:10:04,890 --> 00:10:08,350
Next: how fast is
photosynthesis happening?

211
00:10:08,350 --> 00:10:09,870
That's its rate.

212
00:10:09,870 --> 00:10:13,620
To measure it, use
pondweed, a water plant.

213
00:10:13,620 --> 00:10:17,420
Put it in water with a little
sodium hydrogencarbonate,

214
00:10:17,420 --> 00:10:19,780
which supplies carbon dioxide.

215
00:10:19,780 --> 00:10:25,190
In the light, the pondweed gives
off bubbles of gas, mostly oxygen.

216
00:10:25,490 --> 00:10:27,890
Count the bubbles per minute.

217
00:10:27,890 --> 00:10:30,270
Or, for more accurate results,

218
00:10:30,270 --> 00:10:33,090
collect the gas and
measure its volume,

219
00:10:33,090 --> 00:10:36,180
because bubbles can
be different sizes.

220
00:10:36,680 --> 00:10:38,870
To change the light intensity,

221
00:10:38,870 --> 00:10:41,750
move a lamp closer or further away.

222
00:10:41,750 --> 00:10:43,350
The closer the lamp,

223
00:10:43,350 --> 00:10:45,850
the higher the light intensity.

224
00:10:45,850 --> 00:10:49,570
So, light intensity is
the independent variable.

225
00:10:49,570 --> 00:10:52,360
The rate is the dependent variable.

226
00:10:52,360 --> 00:10:55,410
And everything else
must be kept constant:

227
00:10:55,410 --> 00:10:59,260
the same plant, the same
carbon dioxide concentration,

228
00:10:59,260 --> 00:11:01,370
and the same temperature.

229
00:11:01,370 --> 00:11:02,870
An LED lamp helps,

230
00:11:02,870 --> 00:11:07,310
because it gives off much less
heat than a filament bulb.

231
00:11:07,610 --> 00:11:09,260
At each new distance,

232
00:11:09,260 --> 00:11:11,930
wait a few minutes before you count,

233
00:11:11,930 --> 00:11:14,080
so the plant can adjust.

234
00:11:14,080 --> 00:11:17,110
Then repeat, and calculate a mean.

235
00:11:18,260 --> 00:11:20,170
Here are the results.

236
00:11:20,170 --> 00:11:22,220
As light intensity increases,

237
00:11:22,220 --> 00:11:25,020
the rate of photosynthesis increases.

238
00:11:25,020 --> 00:11:26,930
Then it levels off.

239
00:11:27,230 --> 00:11:30,020
Increase the carbon
dioxide concentration,

240
00:11:30,020 --> 00:11:32,230
and you get the same shape:

241
00:11:32,230 --> 00:11:33,690
the rate increases,

242
00:11:33,690 --> 00:11:35,360
then levels off.

243
00:11:35,660 --> 00:11:37,680
Temperature is different.

244
00:11:37,680 --> 00:11:40,350
The rate increases up to an optimum,

245
00:11:40,350 --> 00:11:42,070
then falls quickly.

246
00:11:42,070 --> 00:11:47,020
That's because the reactions of
photosynthesis are catalysed by enzymes.

247
00:11:47,020 --> 00:11:48,430
Below the optimum,

248
00:11:48,430 --> 00:11:52,680
raising the temperature gives the
molecules more kinetic energy,

249
00:11:52,680 --> 00:11:57,370
so there are more frequent effective
collisions between enzymes and their

250
00:11:57,370 --> 00:11:59,920
substrates. Above the optimum,

251
00:11:59,920 --> 00:12:02,080
the enzymes are denatured.

252
00:12:02,080 --> 00:12:04,370
Their active sites change shape,

253
00:12:04,370 --> 00:12:07,110
so the substrates no longer fit.

254
00:12:07,610 --> 00:12:10,380
When a question says
describe a graph,

255
00:12:10,380 --> 00:12:14,040
say what happens, with
numbers from the axes.

256
00:12:14,040 --> 00:12:16,870
When it says explain, say why.

257
00:12:18,020 --> 00:12:19,770
For the Extended paper,

258
00:12:19,770 --> 00:12:24,180
you need to explain those
shapes using limiting factors.

259
00:12:24,180 --> 00:12:29,090
A limiting factor is something in
short supply that limits the rate of a

260
00:12:29,090 --> 00:12:30,180
process.

261
00:12:30,680 --> 00:12:32,830
Back to the light graph.

262
00:12:32,830 --> 00:12:34,430
On this rising part,

263
00:12:34,430 --> 00:12:37,960
the rate increases as
light intensity increases.

264
00:12:37,960 --> 00:12:41,190
So light intensity is
the limiting factor.

265
00:12:41,190 --> 00:12:42,690
On this flat part,

266
00:12:42,690 --> 00:12:45,190
more light makes no difference.

267
00:12:45,190 --> 00:12:47,540
Light is no longer limiting.

268
00:12:47,540 --> 00:12:48,950
Something else is,

269
00:12:48,950 --> 00:12:52,780
like carbon dioxide
concentration, or temperature.

270
00:12:53,280 --> 00:12:58,030
Now repeat the experiment at a
higher carbon dioxide concentration.

271
00:12:58,030 --> 00:12:59,780
The curve rises higher,

272
00:12:59,780 --> 00:13:01,740
and levels off higher.

273
00:13:01,740 --> 00:13:06,770
That shows carbon dioxide was the
limiting factor on the flat part.

274
00:13:07,270 --> 00:13:10,230
In the exam, name
the limiting factor,

275
00:13:10,230 --> 00:13:11,910
and give the reason:

276
00:13:11,910 --> 00:13:14,610
increasing it increases the rate.

277
00:13:15,110 --> 00:13:18,800
Different conditions have
different limiting factors.

278
00:13:18,800 --> 00:13:21,160
At dawn, or on a dull day,

279
00:13:21,160 --> 00:13:24,640
light intensity is usually
the limiting factor.

280
00:13:24,640 --> 00:13:26,430
On a cold winter's day,

281
00:13:26,430 --> 00:13:28,300
it's often temperature.

282
00:13:28,300 --> 00:13:31,060
And on a bright, warm summer's day,

283
00:13:31,060 --> 00:13:34,060
it's often carbon
dioxide concentration.

284
00:13:35,210 --> 00:13:36,930
One more practical.

285
00:13:36,930 --> 00:13:41,130
Hydrogencarbonate indicator shows
how much carbon dioxide is in a

286
00:13:41,130 --> 00:13:46,550
solution. At the normal level of
carbon dioxide in air, it's red.

287
00:13:46,550 --> 00:13:48,400
With more carbon dioxide,

288
00:13:48,400 --> 00:13:49,970
it turns yellow.

289
00:13:49,970 --> 00:13:51,820
With less carbon dioxide,

290
00:13:51,820 --> 00:13:53,490
it turns purple.

291
00:13:53,990 --> 00:13:57,170
Here are three sealed
tubes of indicator.

292
00:13:57,170 --> 00:14:00,090
One has a leaf, in bright light.

293
00:14:00,090 --> 00:14:02,750
One has a leaf, wrapped in foil,

294
00:14:02,750 --> 00:14:04,650
so it's in the dark.

295
00:14:04,650 --> 00:14:07,430
And one has no leaf at all.

296
00:14:07,430 --> 00:14:09,710
What colour will each tube be,

297
00:14:09,710 --> 00:14:11,570
after a few hours?

298
00:14:12,070 --> 00:14:15,590
Pause the video, and
make your prediction.

299
00:14:19,940 --> 00:14:21,960
Here's what happens.

300
00:14:21,960 --> 00:14:25,330
In the light, the
indicator turns purple.

301
00:14:25,330 --> 00:14:28,120
Photosynthesis is faster
than respiration,

302
00:14:28,120 --> 00:14:32,750
so the leaf takes in more carbon
dioxide than it gives out.

303
00:14:33,050 --> 00:14:35,730
In the dark, it turns yellow.

304
00:14:35,730 --> 00:14:37,490
There's no photosynthesis,

305
00:14:37,490 --> 00:14:39,440
but respiration carries on,

306
00:14:39,440 --> 00:14:42,520
so the leaf gives out carbon dioxide.

307
00:14:42,820 --> 00:14:45,550
The tube with no leaf stays red.

308
00:14:45,550 --> 00:14:47,220
That's the control.

309
00:14:47,220 --> 00:14:51,080
It shows that the leaf
caused the colour changes.

310
00:14:51,380 --> 00:14:54,960
And in dim light, the
two rates can be equal,

311
00:14:54,960 --> 00:14:57,360
so the indicator stays red.

312
00:14:57,860 --> 00:14:59,980
Which busts a classic myth:

313
00:14:59,980 --> 00:15:03,470
that plants photosynthesise
instead of respiring.

314
00:15:03,470 --> 00:15:05,560
Plants respire all the time,

315
00:15:05,560 --> 00:15:08,240
day and night, just like you.

316
00:15:08,240 --> 00:15:11,650
In bright light,
photosynthesis is just faster,

317
00:15:11,650 --> 00:15:14,920
so there's a net uptake
of carbon dioxide.

318
00:15:14,920 --> 00:15:17,900
Net is the word examiners look for.

319
00:15:19,050 --> 00:15:21,880
So, back to van Helmont's willow.

320
00:15:21,880 --> 00:15:25,510
Where did those seventy-four
kilograms come from?

321
00:15:25,910 --> 00:15:28,240
Carbon dioxide, from the air,

322
00:15:28,240 --> 00:15:30,630
and water, from the soil.

323
00:15:30,630 --> 00:15:32,140
Joined into glucose,

324
00:15:32,140 --> 00:15:33,940
using energy from light,

325
00:15:33,940 --> 00:15:35,840
then built into cellulose,

326
00:15:35,840 --> 00:15:37,710
starch and proteins.

327
00:15:38,010 --> 00:15:41,850
The little that the soil lost
was mostly mineral ions,

328
00:15:41,850 --> 00:15:44,160
like nitrate and magnesium.

329
00:15:44,560 --> 00:15:48,220
Van Helmont thought his
tree was made of water.

330
00:15:48,220 --> 00:15:50,420
He was only partly right.

331
00:15:50,420 --> 00:15:53,100
A living tree is full of water.

332
00:15:53,100 --> 00:15:57,230
But most of the wood itself
was built from carbon dioxide,

333
00:15:57,230 --> 00:15:59,380
taken out of thin air.

334
00:16:00,530 --> 00:16:02,060
Let's recap.

335
00:16:02,060 --> 00:16:06,780
Photosynthesis: carbon dioxide plus
water gives glucose plus oxygen,

336
00:16:06,780 --> 00:16:09,960
in the presence of
light and chlorophyll.

337
00:16:09,960 --> 00:16:14,520
Chlorophyll transfers energy from
light into energy in chemicals.

338
00:16:14,520 --> 00:16:16,810
Glucose is used for respiration,

339
00:16:16,810 --> 00:16:18,320
starch, cellulose,

340
00:16:18,320 --> 00:16:20,040
sucrose and nectar.

341
00:16:20,040 --> 00:16:22,490
Nitrate ions make amino acids.

342
00:16:22,490 --> 00:16:24,950
Magnesium ions make chlorophyll.

343
00:16:24,950 --> 00:16:26,890
Leaves are broad, thin,

344
00:16:26,890 --> 00:16:28,490
and built in layers,

345
00:16:28,490 --> 00:16:30,950
each adapted for photosynthesis.

346
00:16:30,950 --> 00:16:35,940
Light, carbon dioxide and temperature
can all be limiting factors.

347
00:16:36,440 --> 00:16:38,700
And that's plant nutrition.
